    {
      "id": "scene_pLcv6GIZoX",
      "stageId": "Di2g81UobE",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Check Your Understanding: Basics",
      "order": 3,
      "content": {
        "type": "quiz",
        "questions": [
          {
            "id": "q1",
            "type": "single",
            "question": "What is the correct command to print 'Hello, World!' to the screen in Python?",
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "A",
                "label": "print('Hello, World!')"
              },
              {
                "value": "B",
                "label": "echo('Hello, World!')"
              },
              {
                "value": "C",
                "label": "console.log('Hello, World!')"
              },
              {
                "value": "D",
                "label": "write('Hello, World!')"
              }
            ],
            "answer": [
              "A"
            ],
            "analysis": "In Python, the built-in function to output text is `print()`. `echo()` is used in PHP, `console.log()` is used in JavaScript, and `write()` is not a Python built-in for console output. Therefore, only option A is correct.",
            "points": 10,
            "hasAnswer": true
          },
          {
            "id": "q2",
            "type": "short_answer",
            "question": "In your own words, explain what an interpreter does when you run a Python program. Include at least one example.",
            "commentPrompt": "Rubric: (1) Describes that the interpreter reads and executes Python code line by line - 40% (2) Mentions that it translates Python into machine-readable instructions OR that it checks for errors - 30% (3) Provides a clear example, e.g., when you run `print(\"Hello\")`, the interpreter shows the output - 30%",
            "analysis": "A Python interpreter reads your code step by step from top to bottom. It translates each line into instructions the computer can understand and runs them immediately. For example, when you type `print('Hello')` and run the program, the interpreter sees that command and outputs 'Hello' on the screen. If there is a mistake, like a typo, the interpreter often stops and shows an error message.",
            "points": 20,
            "hasAnswer": false
          }
        ],
        "knowledgePoints": [
          "Python uses the `print()` function to display text on the screen.",
          "An interpreter executes Python code line by line, translating it into machine instructions.",
          "A simple first program in Python is `print('Hello, World!')`.",
          "The interpreter helps catch errors by stopping and showing error messages when something is wrong.",
          "Python is a high-level language designed to be easy to read and write for beginners."
        ]
      },
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_o4WX3aWz",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Great job, everyone! Now let's check your understanding of the basics we just covered."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_D-BjHuZK",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "First question: What is the correct command to print 'Hello, World!' to the screen in Python? The answer is A: print('Hello, World!'). In Python, we use the `print()` function to display text, unlike other languages that use `echo` or `console.log`."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_gxc9tLaH",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Now for the second question: a short answer about what an interpreter does. The Python interpreter reads your code line by line, translates it into machine code, and runs it immediately. For example, when you write `print('Hello, World!')`, the interpreter sends that text to your screen."
        },
        {
          "id": "action_UHmfQMLG",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "Think about your own explanation. Did you mention that the interpreter checks for errors as it runs? Or that it's different from a compiler which translates all code at once? These are key points!"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_EBv41oyu",
          "type": "speech",
          "text": "If you got both right, you're already building a solid foundation in Python. If not, don't worry—the examples and practice ahead will help it all stick."
        }
      ],
